A||N||3||Bugsy||Another cooling fan question||||5||
Z||000000||Bugsy||07-29-2000||11:00 AM||||When my car was in the shop having the body repaired and painted, I had the mechanic pull the engine to drill out some broken manifold bolts. I discovered yesterday that the fan isn't coming on at 200 degrees like it used to. If I hadn't looked at the temp gauge I wouldn't have noticed that my engine was on the virge of overheating.  If he forgot to re-connect the sensor (hopefully), where do I look for it?<P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||5
Z||000001||Robert||07-29-2000||11:35 AM||||It's just to the right of the water filler on the front of the engine.  It has a single wire lead.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-29-2000||02:29 PM||||Actually, the cooling fan don't kick on until 230-235 degrees. Turn your A/C switch on, that is supposed to automatically turn on the fan. If the fan does work, then at least that tells you the fan is connected and has power going to it. There are three coolant temp sensors/sending units: temp gauge, coolant fan, and ECM sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bugsy||07-30-2000||06:55 PM||||Thanx guys, <BR>...Robert, I'll check on that wire in the morning  thanx<BR>...Batboy, I knew when I posted the question, that I'd be corrected. I just couldn't remember what the temp gauge read when the cooling fan kicked on. There was damage to the fan in the accident, it still worked but it was NOISY, darn thing vibrated the whole car. The body repairs were finished early June I think, and rarely turn off the air. We are having some unseasonal cool weather, so I'm not using the air. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||6
